nalyst_adjustment": -0.05, "key_factors": ["Redistricting added Stockton to CA-13, making the seat more Democratic for incumbent Adam Gray", "Gray holds a large financial edge: $4.2M raised and $2.1M on hand versus Kevin Lincoln's $2.2M and $0.8M", "All ten expert ratings favor Democrats (Tilt D to Safe D), and prediction markets price Gray at 96-99%", "No public general-election polling exists, leaving the forecast to rest on ratings and fundamentals"], "rationale": "The evidence converges on a Democratic edge: the redrawn district leans Democratic, Gray outspends Lincoln by roughly two-to-one, every major rater places the race on the Democratic side, and markets price Gray near certain. With no polls to contradict that picture, a Likely D rating with a double-digit expected margin fits the evidence.", "watch": "Sustained Republican investment in Lincoln, including more outside spending or national attention like Mike Johnson's visit, or any polling showing the race tighter than ratings suggest, could pull this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Adam Gray (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Adam Gray, the Democratic incumbent, faces Republican Kevin Lincoln, a former mayor, in a Central Valley district that redistricting reshaped by adding Stockton. The seat leans slightly Democratic on paper, and Gray enters with a clear financial edge, having raised about $4.2 million to Lincoln's $2.2 million while holding more than twice his cash on hand. Outside spending has been minimal on both sides so far.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and outside assessments. Rating outlets uniformly place the race on the Democratic side, ranging from Tilt D to Safe D with a consensus near Likely D, and prediction markets price Gray's chances in the mid-to-high 90s. Speaker Mike Johnson's visit to stump for Lincoln and a critical story about Gray's campaign spending are the notable wrinkles, but neither has visibly moved the race. Gray is favored, though his cushion is moderate rather than overwhelming.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9502, "margin": 10.95}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9343, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.945, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.941, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9142, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9277, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9289, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9426, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9484, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9504, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9439, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9519, "margin": 11.0}]}